# Web UI Context

Four Next.js 15 apps under `web/`:

| App | Path | Port | Auth |
|-----|------|------|------|
| Public website | `website/` | 3000 | Public only |
| Admin dashboard | `admin/` | 3001 | `POST /auth/login/admin` |
| Customer portal | `customer/` | 3002 | `POST /auth/login/customer` |
| Employee portal | `employee/` | 3003 | `POST /auth/login/employee` |

API base: `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` → `http://localhost:8080/api/v1`

# Source of truth

- Screen list + APIs: `docs/UI_INVENTORY.md`
- OpenAPI spec: `internal/app/spec/openapi.yaml`
- Deferred features (no UI): `docs/DEFERRED_SCOPE.md`

# Patterns (copy from `web/admin`)

- `lib/api-client.ts` — typed fetch with JWT + envelope parsing
- `lib/auth.ts` — role-specific cookie + localStorage (never share tokens across apps)
- `middleware.ts` — protect portal routes; public auth pages only
- `services/*.ts` — API calls only (no React)
- `hooks/use-*.ts` — TanStack Query wrappers
- Handlers stay thin; business logic stays in the Go API

# Stack

Tailwind CSS, shadcn-style UI in `components/ui/`, TanStack Query, React Hook Form, Zod, lucide-react.

Use the **shadcn MCP** to install missing components (`table`, `dialog`, `calendar`, `tabs`, etc.) instead of hand-rolling. If an app has no `components.json`, run `npx shadcn@latest init` in that app first, or copy existing primitives from `web/admin/components/ui/`.

# App boundaries

- **website/** — marketing + discovery only; link "Book" / "Sign in" to customer app (3002)
- **customer/** — bookings, profile, addresses, chat, notifications
- **employee/** — services, availability, booking inbox, KYC, subscription
- **admin/** — moderation, analytics, platform settings

Do not invent API endpoints. Do not build UI for deferred backend scope (customer checkout, wallet, payouts, OTP, social login).

# New page checklist

1. Find screen ID in `docs/UI_INVENTORY.md`
2. Add route under correct app
3. Add service function + hook
4. Reuse `DataTable`, `ConfirmDialog`, `Pagination` from admin where applicable
5. Match existing naming, types in `types/`, and error display patterns
